      "body": "## Finding Description and Impact\n\n`WERC7575ShareToken` enforces KYC on the standard user flows (`transfer`, `transferFrom`, `mint`, `burn`) by reverting when the recipient (and for mint/burn the holder) is not marked `isKycVerified`. The validator-only settlement helpers `batchTransfers` and `rBatchTransfers` bypass this gate: they directly mutate `_balances` (and `_rBalances` for rBatch) for arbitrary debtor/creditor pairs without checking `isKycVerified` and without `whenNotPaused`. \n\nA validator can therefore move funds from KYC-verified addresses to any non-KYC (or sanctioned) address in one call, defeating the on-chain compliance guarantee. Once received, those non-KYC addresses can route funds onward to KYC accounts using normal transfers (which only check the recipient), making this a practical, single key compliance bypass rather than a theoretical edge case. Pausing is also ineffective against the validator batch paths because they omit `whenNotPaused`.\n\nThis vulnerability is a KYC enforcement and access control logic flaw in the `WERC7575ShareToken` contract’s validator-only batch transfer mechanisms. The token enforces KYC checks via the isKycVerified mapping in its standard `transfer`, `transferFrom`, `mint`, and `burn` functions, but the `batchTransfers` and `rBatchTransfers` functions, which are restricted to the validator role, directly mutate `_balances` and `_rBalances` without consulting isKycVerified. As a result, these privileged batch operations bypass the on-chain KYC gating applied to normal user flows.\n\n### Affected code\n- [`src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol:472-491`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-11-sukukfi/blob/main/src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol#L472-L491)  Standard `transfer` enforces `isKycVerified[to]` under `whenNotPaused`.\n- [`src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol:700-734`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-11-sukukfi/blob/main/src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol#L700-L734)  `batchTransfers` mutates `_balances` with no KYC or pause check.\n- [`src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol:1119-1183`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2025-11-sukukfi/blob/main/src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol#L1119-L1183)  `rBatchTransfers` mutates `_balances`/`_rBalances` with no KYC or pause check.\n\n```solidity\n// src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol Lines 472-478\nfunction transfer(address to, uint256 value) public override whenNotPaused returns (bool) {\n    address from = msg.sender;\n    if (!isKycVerified[to]) revert KycRequired();\n    _spendAllowance(from, from, value);\n    return super.transfer(to, value);\n}\n```\n- This standard transfer function enforces the KYC policy: it requires `whenNotPaused` and explicitly reverts if the recipient `to` is not marked `isKycVerified`. This establishes the intended invariant that all token transfers must be KYC-gated, which the validator batch functions later violate.\n\n\n\n\n```solidity\n// src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol:700-717\nfunction batchTransfers(address[] calldata debtors, address[] calldata creditors, uint256[] calldata amounts) external onlyValidator returns (bool) {\n    (DebitAndCredit[] memory accounts, uint256 accountsLength) = consolidateTransfers(debtors, creditors, amounts);\n\n    // CEI: Update balances only (do NOT modify rBalances - that is rBatchTransfers' job)\n    for (uint256 i = 0; i < accountsLength;) {\n        DebitAndCredit memory account = accounts[i];\n        if (account.debit > account.credit) {\n            uint256 amount = account.debit - account.credit;\n            uint256 debtorBalance = _balances[account.owner];\n            if (debtorBalance < amount) revert LowBalance();\n            unchecked {\n                _balances[account.owner] -= amount;\n            }\n        } else if (account.debit < account.credit) {\n            uint256 amount = account.credit - account.debit;\n            unchecked {\n                _balances[account.owner] += amount;\n            }\n        }\n```\n- The `batchTransfers` validator-only function directly debits and credits `_balances` for multiple accounts without any `isKycVerified` checks and without `whenNotPaused`. A compromised/malicious validator can use this to move balances between arbitrary addresses, including non‑KYCed ones, bypassing the KYC restrictions applied in `transfer`/`transferFrom`.\n\n\n```solidity\n// src/WERC7575ShareToken.sol Lines 1119-1136\nfunction rBatchTransfers(address[] calldata debtors, address[] calldata creditors, uint256[] calldata amounts, uint256 rBalanceFlags) external onlyValidator returns (bool) {\n    // PHASE 2A: Consolidate transfers into aggregated accounts\n    // Same aggregation as computeRBalanceFlags: N transfers → M unique accounts (M <= 2N)\n    // Account order matches rBalanceFlags bitmap indices\n    (DebitAndCredit[] memory accounts, uint256 accountsLength) = consolidateTransfers(debtors, creditors, amounts);\n\n    // PHASE 2B: Update balances with Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern\n    // Check: Verify sufficient balance BEFORE state change\n    // Effects: Update _balances and _rBalances\n    // Interactions: Emit events AFTER state is finalized\n    for (uint256 i = 0; i < accountsLength;) {\n        DebitAndCredit memory account = accounts[i];\n\n        if (account.debit > account.credit) {\n            // CASE 1: Account is net DEBTOR (losing tokens)\n            // This account had more outflows than inflows\n            uint256 amount = account.debit - account.credit;\n```\n- The `rBatchTransfers` validator-only function similarly consolidates transfers and iterates over aggregated accounts to update balances, again with no KYC verification on recipients or participants. This snippet shows the function entry and loop that processes accounts, illustrating that privileged batch settlement logic can mutate balances (and later `_rBalances`) without enforcing the on-chain KYC gating used in normal flows.\n---\n## Impact\n\n- **KYC bypass by a single key**: Validator can route funds to unverified/sanctioned destinations in one call, nullifying the compliance invariant.\n- **Pause bypass**: `batchTransfers`/`rBatchTransfers` ignore `whenNotPaused`, allowing fund movement even while the token is paused.\n- **Regulatory/operational risk**: On-chain KYC guarantees become unreliable; downstream integrators relying on transfer gating are exposed.\n\n---\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n1. Add `whenNotPaused` and `if (!isKycVerified[creditor]) revert KycRequired();` (and the analogous debtor check if policy requires) to `batchTransfers` and `rBatchTransfers`.\n2. Validate KYC for every participant in consolidated transfers (both debtor and creditor) before mutating balances.\n3. Consider emitting explicit events for KYC failures on validator paths to aid monitoring and incident response.\n\n---",
